== Chapter VII

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Showing the nature, ground, kinds, properties, and degree of love to God.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

2\. I proceed to the second general branch of the text: the #emph[persons] who have a share in this privilege. They are qualified in two ways.

1\. They are #emph[lovers] of God: “all things work together for good τοῖς ἀγαπῶσι τὸν θεὸν, to those who love God.” Those who despise and hate God have no #emph[lot] or #emph[part] in this privilege. It is “the children’s bread”; it belongs only to those who #emph[love God.] Because love is the very heart and spirit of religion, I will enlarge on it all the more. To discuss it further, I will make these five things clear.

#[
#set enum(indent: 24pt)
+ The nature of love to God.
+ The ground of love to God.
+ The kinds of love to God.
+ The properties of love to God.
+ The degree of love to God.
]

=== The Nature of Love

1\. The #emph[nature] of divine love. Love is an expansion of the soul, or the kindling of the affections, by which a Christian breathes after God as the supreme and sovereign good. Augustine, in his second volume, calls love #emph[pondus animae] \[the weight of the soul\]. Love is to the soul what the weights are to a clock: it sets the soul going toward God. It is the wing by which we fly to heaven. By love we cling to God, as the needle clings to the magnet.

=== The Ground of Love

2\. The #emph[ground] of love to God is #emph[knowledge. Ignoti nulla cupido]: we cannot love what we do not know. For our love to be drawn out to God, we must know these three things in him.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. A #emph[fullness] (Col 1:19). He has a fullness of grace to cleanse us and of glory to crown us. It is a fullness not only of #emph[sufficiency] but of #emph[overflowing.] He is a sea of goodness without bottom and banks.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. A #emph[freeness. Fluit acrius amne perenni] \[it flows more freely than an ever-running river\]. God has an inborn readiness to give out mercy and grace. He drops like the honeycomb (Rev 22:17): “Let the one who desires the water of life drink freely.” God does not require us to bring money with us, only appetite.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. A #emph[propriety,] that is, ownership. We must know that this fullness in God is ours (Ps 48:14): “This God is our God.” Here is the ground of love: #emph[Deity] and #emph[propriety.]]]

=== The Kinds of Love

3\. The #emph[kinds] of love. I will divide them into these three.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. There is a love of #emph[appreciation,] when we set a high value on God as the most sublime and infinite good. We so esteem God that if we have him, we do not care though we lack everything else. The stars vanish when the sun appears. So all creatures vanish in our thoughts when the Sun of righteousness shines in his full splendor (Song 1:13): “My beloved is to me a sachet of myrrh,” and “a cluster of henna blossoms.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. A love of #emph[complacency] and delight. So #emph[Aquinas] defines love as #emph[complacentia amantis in amato] \[the delight of the lover in the beloved\], as a man takes delight in a friend whom he loves. The soul that loves God rejoices in him as its treasure and rests in him as its center. The heart is so set on God that it desires nothing more (John 14:8): “Show us the Father, and that will be enough for us.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. A love of #emph[benevolence,] which is wishing well to the cause of God#footnote[Φιλεῖν ἐστι βούλεσθαι τινὶ ὁ ὄντα ἀγαθὰ, etc. \[To love is to wish for someone what one thinks good.\] #emph[Aristotle.]]. He who is bound in affection to his friend wishes him every happiness. This is loving God: when we are his well-wishers and desire that his interest may prevail. Our vote and prayer is that his name may be held in honor, and that his gospel, which is “the rod of his strength,” may, like #emph[Aaron’s rod,] blossom and bring forth almonds.]]

=== The Properties of Love

4\. The #emph[properties] of love.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. Our love to God must be #emph[entire,] and that #emph[ex parte subjecti,] with regard to the #emph[subject:] it must be with the whole heart (Mark 12:30): “Love the Lord your God ἐξ ὅλης καρδίας, with all your heart.” Under the old law, a high priest was not to marry a widow or a harlot: not a widow, because he did not have her first love, and not a harlot, because he did not have all her love. God will have the whole heart. Hos 10:2: “Their heart is divided.” The true mother would not have the child divided, and God will not have the heart divided. God will not be a #emph[lodger,] with only one room in the heart while all the other rooms are let out to sin. It must be an entire love.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. It must be a #emph[sincere] love (Eph 6:24): “Grace to all who love our Lord Jesus ἐν ἀφθαρσίᾳ, in sincerity.” Sincere, #emph[quasi sine cera] \[as if without wax\]: it alludes to honey that is pure and has no wax in it. Our love to God is sincere when it is #emph[pure] and free of self-interest. The schoolmen call this #emph[amor amicitiae, a love of friendship.] We must love Christ #emph[propter Christum] (as Augustine says), for #emph[himself,] as we love sweet wine for its taste. God’s beauty and love must be the two magnets that draw our love to him. #emph[Alexander] had two friends, #emph[Hephaestion] and #emph[Craterus.] “#emph[Hephaestion,]” he said, “loves me because I am #emph[Alexander;] #emph[Craterus] loves me because I am #emph[King Alexander.]” The one loved his person, the other his benefits. Many love God because he gives them grain and wine, and not for his own excellencies. #emph[Lycurgus] wanted young women to be married without a dowry, so that their husbands would marry them purely for love. We must love God more for what he is than for what he gives. True love is not mercenary. You do not need to hire a mother to love her child. A soul deeply in love with God does not need to be hired with rewards; he cannot help loving him for the radiant beauty that sparkles forth in him.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[3\. It must be a #emph[fervent] love. The Hebrew word for #emph[love,] אהבא, signifies ardent affection. Saints must be seraphim, burning in holy love. To love someone coldly is the same as not to love him. The sun shines as hot as it can. Our love to God must be intense and vehement, like the “burning coals of the broom tree,” which are the hottest and fiercest (Ps 120:4). Our love to passing things must be indifferent; we must love #emph[quasi non,] “as if we did not” (1 Cor 7:30)#footnote[#emph[Non jubet Apostolus ut mundana haec abjiciamus, sed ne simus hisce fluxis rebus immersi; tunc enim non possidemus, sed ab illis possidemur.] \[The apostle does not command us to throw these worldly things away, but not to be sunk in these passing things; for then we do not possess them, but are possessed by them.\] Beza.]. But our love to God must flame forth. The bride was #emph[amore perculsa,] “faint with love” for Christ (Song 2:5). We can never love God as he deserves. As God’s punishing us is less than we deserve (Ezra 9:13), so our loving him is less than he deserves.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[4\. Love to God must be #emph[active.] It is like fire, the most active element. It is called “your labor of love” (1 Thess 1:3). Love is no idle grace. It sets the head studying for God and the feet running in the ways of his commandments: “Christ’s love compels” (2 Cor 5:14). Claims of love are not enough. True love is seen not only at the tongue’s end but at the fingers’ end; it is “the labor of love.” Those living creatures in Ezek 1:8 had wings, and hands under their wings. That is an emblem of a good Christian: he has not only the wings of faith to fly with, but hands under his wings. #emph[He works by love;] he #emph[spends and is spent for Christ.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[5\. Love is #emph[liberal.] It has love-tokens to give (1 Cor 13:4): “Love χρηστεύεται, is kind.” Love has not only a smooth tongue but a kind heart. #emph[David’s] heart was on fire with love to God, and #emph[he would not offer to God what cost him nothing] (2 Sam 24:24). Love is full not only of #emph[benevolence] but of #emph[beneficence.] Love that enlarges the heart never tightens the hand. He who loves Christ will be generous to his members. He will be “eyes to the blind” and “feet to the lame.” The backs and bellies of the poor will be the furrows where he sows the golden seeds of generosity. Some say they love God, but their love is lame in one hand: they give nothing to good uses. Faith does indeed deal with things invisible, but God hates love that is invisible. Love is like new wine, which must have a vent; it vents itself in good works. The apostle speaks to the honor of the #emph[Corinthians] that they gave to the poor saints not only #emph[according to] their ability but #emph[beyond] it (2 Cor 8:3). Love is bred at court; it is a noble, bountiful grace.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[6\. Love to God is #emph[peculiar,] that is, set apart for him alone. He who loves God gives him a love that he gives to no one else. God gives his children a love he does not give to the wicked, an #emph[electing, adopting] love. In the same way a gracious heart gives God a special, distinguishing love that no one else shares in (2 Cor 11:2): “I betrothed you#super[+] to one husband, to present you#super[+] as a pure virgin to Christ.” A wife betrothed to a husband gives him a love she has for no one else; she does not part with her married love to anyone but her husband. So a saint betrothed to Christ gives him a love peculiar to him, a love that cannot be shared with any other: a love joined with adoration. Not only the #emph[love] is given to God, but the #emph[soul] (Song 4:12): “My sister, my bride, you are a garden locked up.” The heart of a believer is Christ’s garden. The flower growing in it is love mixed with divine worship, and this flower is for Christ alone to smell. The bride keeps the key of the garden, so that no one may come there but Christ.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[7\. Love to God is #emph[permanent.] It is like the fire the Vestal Virgins kept in #emph[Rome:] it does not go out. True love boils over, but it does not give over. As love to God is sincere without hypocrisy, so it is constant without apostasy. Love is like the pulse of the body, always beating. It is not a flash flood but a spring. Wicked men are constant in their love to their sins: neither shame, nor sickness, nor fear of hell will make them give up their sins. So nothing can hinder a Christian’s love to God. Nothing can conquer love, no difficulties or opposition (Song 8:6): “Love is as strong as the grave.” The grave swallows up the strongest bodies, and love swallows up the strongest difficulties (Song 8:7): “Mighty waters cannot quench love,” neither the sweet waters of pleasure nor the bitter waters of persecution. Love to God holds firm to death (Eph 3:17): “being rooted and grounded in love.” Light things, like chaff and feathers, are quickly blown away, but a tree that is rooted stands through the storm. He who is rooted in love endures. True love never ends except with life.]]

=== The Degrees of Love

5\. The #emph[degrees] of love. We must love God above all other objects#footnote[#emph[Minus Domine te amat, qui aliquid tecum amat.] \[He loves you less, Lord, who loves anything along with you.\] Augustine.] (Ps 73:25): “On earth I desire no one besides You.” God is the essence of all good things; he is good in the highest degree. Now the soul sees a surpassing excellence in God, and wonders at that constellation of all excellencies in him, and so it is carried out in love to him in the highest degree. “The measure of our love to God,” says #emph[Bernard,] “is to love him without measure”#footnote[#emph[Modus diligendi Deum, est diligere Deum sine modo.] Bernard.]. God, who is the chief of our happiness, must have the chief of our affections (Song 8:2): “I would give you spiced wine to drink, the nectar of my pomegranates.” If the bride has any love better than the rest, a cup more juicy and spiced, Christ shall drink of that. The creature may have the milk of our love, but God must have the cream. Love to God must be above all other things, as oil floats above water.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. We must love God more than #emph[relatives.] Take the case of #emph[Abraham’s] offering up #emph[Isaac.] #emph[Isaac] was the son of his old age; no doubt he loved him wholly and doted on him. But God said, “Abraham, offer up your son.” It was a thing that might seem to baffle not only his #emph[reason] but his #emph[faith,] for the #emph[Messiah] was to come from #emph[Isaac;] if Isaac were cut off, where would the world have a Mediator? Yet such was the strength of #emph[Abraham’s] faith, and the ardor of his love to God, that he would take the sacrificing knife and let out #emph[Isaac’s] blood. Our blessed Savior tells us of #emph[hating] father and mother (Luke 14:26). Christ would not have us unnatural. But if our dearest relatives lie in our way and would hinder us from Christ, we must either step over them or tread on them (Deut 33:9). Though some few drops of love may run aside to our kindred and in-laws, the full torrent must run out after Christ. Relatives may lie in the #emph[bosom,] but Christ must lie in the #emph[heart.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[2\. We must love God more than #emph[possessions.] Heb 10:34: “You#super[+] joyfully accepted the confiscation of your property.” They were glad they had anything to lose for Christ. If the world is laid in one scale and Christ in the other, he must weigh heaviest. And is it so with us? Does God have the highest room in our affections? #emph[Plutarch] says that when a dictator was appointed in #emph[Rome,] all other authority was suspended for the time. So when the love of God bears sway in the heart, all other love is suspended, and is as nothing compared with this love.]]
